<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Avaldsnes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Avaldsnes is a historic site on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmoy">Karmøy</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, overlooking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmsund">Karmsund</a> strait. Its great burial mounds and ship burials show that powerful kings controlled the shipping lane there from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and the sagas make it a royal seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>. Its stone church of St Olaf dates from about 1250.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/avaldsnes.jpg" alt="Avaldsnes" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johan Christian Dahl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dahl.I.C.1820.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Avaldsnes lies on the northeastern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmoy">Karmøy</a>, just south of the town of Haugesund in Rogaland county. At this point the coastal shipping route is forced into a narrow passage along the shore, which probably explains the power and wealth that gathered there through the ages. The place is believed to be named after King Augvald, who according to tradition had his seat around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmsund">Karmsundet</a> strait.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> chose Avaldsnes for his main royal estate in about 870, and it has been called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s oldest capital. In 953 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-the-good">Haakon the Good</a> defeated the sons of his half-brother Eirik Bloodaxe at the Battle of Blodeheia, about a kilometre west of the church. According to legend Olav Trygvason built the first church, probably a small stave church serving the royal residence. King Håkon Håkonsson ordered the present St Olav's Church, which was not finished until nearly 1320. It was one of only four royal collegiate churches in Norway and a station on the pilgrim route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidaros-cathedral">Nidaros Cathedral</a>.</p><p>The site holds the only Norwegian example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> burial mounds in a row. The ship burial at Storhaug, excavated from 1886, held an oak vessel walled in with stone, and Grønhaug, examined by Haakon Shetelig in 1902, held a boat about 15 metres long with a man's grave of the 10th century. Flagghaugen, levelled in 1835, proved to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s richest grave of the Roman period, with a gold neck ring of 600 grams, weapons and Roman vessels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and bronze.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Avaldsnes is a village of Karmøy Municipality and was the centre of the former Avaldsnes Municipality from 1838 until 1965. Beside the church stands Mary's Needle, a standing stone 7.2 metres high that leans towards the church wall. The Nordvegen History Centre, opened in 2005 and built largely underground, has a reconstructed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> farm on the island of Bukkøy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Avaldsnes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At <b>Avaldsnes</b> on Karmøy, great mounds and a ship burial of the late 8th century mark a seat of kings who controlled shipping through the narrow Karmsund strait along the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/rogaland">Rogaland</a></li></ul></section>
